Serverless doesn't mean there are no servers; it simply means the servers are no longer your problem.
It is the architectural shift from renting an entire factory and paying for the electricity 24/7, to dropping your raw materials into a magic box and 
paying only for the exact seconds it takes to process them.

Technical Breakdown :- 
In a traditional provisioned pipeline, you spin up a cluster (like AWS EMR or an EC2 fleet). You must predict your workload, set auto-scaling rules, manage OS patches, 
and pay for the infrastructure as long as it is turned on.
Serverless data tools—like AWS Glue, Google BigQuery, or AWS Lambda—abstract all of this away. You simply provide the Python script, the Spark job, or the SQL query.
The cloud provider maintains a massive, hidden pool of compute resources. When your pipeline triggers, the provider instantly allocates the exact amount of CPU and RAM needed, runs the code,
returns the output, and immediately tears the environment down. You don't manage nodes; you just execute code.

Buisness Context :- 
This eliminates the 'Idle Tax'. Most enterprise data pipelines are spiky—they run heavy batch jobs at 2 AM and sit completely idle at 2 PM.
With provisioned servers, you pay for that idle time. With serverless, your cloud bill drops to zero the millisecond the pipeline finishes. 
Furthermore, it drastically reduces Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) because highly-paid data engineers stop acting as part-time DevOps administrators.

Design Decision :- 
The fatal flaw of serverless is that it is a financial trap for predictable, heavy workloads. 
While serverless is incredibly cheap for spiky, event-driven ingestion, the compute cost per-second is significantly higher than a provisioned server.
If you have a massive Kafka streaming job that processes 10 terabytes of data continuously 24/7, a serverless architecture will bankrupt your cloud budget.
As a data engineer, I architect hybrid systems: I use serverless Lambda functions to catch unpredictable, low-volume webhook events, but I route the massive, 
predictable nightly transformations to dedicated, reserved-instance Spark clusters to tightly control costs.

In short to handle a spike serverless is a good option.
